/*
  The tests, typechecked.

  Every package's `tsconfig.json` sets `rootDir: src` so that `dist` mirrors
  `src` — which means no package can include its own `test` directory, and for a
  long time none of them did. The consequence was not theoretical. The plot
  fixture in `apps/web/test/screens.test.tsx` was missing `start`, `end` and
  `bearingDeg` on every edge; it was structurally not a `Plot`, it had been
  wrong for as long as it had existed, and nothing said so until the vehicle
  access placement read `edge.start.x` and got `undefined` at runtime.

  A fixture that has drifted from the type it claims to be is worse than no
  fixture: it goes on proving the screens work against a shape the API never
  sends. So this project exists purely to check them — `noEmit`, no `rootDir`,
  every test directory in one pass.

  It is not a composite project and it is not referenced by the solution file,
  because it depends on the built `dist` of everything it imports. `pnpm
  typecheck` runs `tsc -b` first and then this, in that order.
*/
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "composite": false,
    "incremental": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    /*
      The workspace packages, mapped to their built types.

      A test may import any of them, and several do so from an app that has no
      reason to declare the package itself. The mapping makes the compiler
      resolve them the way Vitest's aliases already do, against the same `dist`
      the runtime loads.

      `@envelope/core` used to be the example here — declared by nothing,
      because the browser bundle never imported it. It does now:
      `LevelSchedulePanel` calls `levelCode` and `scheduleRefusal` so that the
      height code on screen is the engine's own string rather than a second
      rendering of it, and the package moved into `apps/web`'s dependencies.
    */
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@envelope/*": ["packages/*/dist/index.d.ts"]
    }
  },
  /*
    `apps/web/src/vite-env.d.ts` is listed because a web test reaches into
    `apps/web/src`, and one module there reads `import.meta.glob`. An ambient
    declaration is only in the program if something includes it, and nothing here
    imports a `.d.ts`.
  */
  "include": [
    "test-support/**/*.ts",
    "packages/*/test/**/*.ts",
    "packages/*/test/**/*.tsx",
    "apps/*/test/**/*.ts",
    "apps/*/test/**/*.tsx",
    "apps/web/src/vite-env.d.ts"
  ]
}
